    {
      "crop_slug": "mint",
      "field_path": "failure_diagnostics[0].what_happened_seasoned",
      "our_prose": "The patch colonized the bed through shallow rhizomes and stolons, the defining mint behavior every extension source warns about (contain it in a sunken bottomless container or lined bed). Runners left unpulled root at the nodes and become hard to eradicate. Next time confine at planting and edge the container above the soil line.",
      "run_normalized": "the container above the soil line",
      "run_in_our_prose": "the container above the soil line",
      "run_words": 6,
      "all_maximal_runs": [
        "the container above the soil line"
      ],
      "maximal_run_count": 1,
      "source_ids": [
        "umd_ext"
      ],
      "source_names": [
        "University of Maryland Extension"
      ],
      "source_publishers": [
        "UMD Extension"
      ],
      "source_tiers": [
        "T1"
      ],
      "source_url": "https://extension.umd.edu/resource/growing-mint-home-garden",
      "anchor_paths": [
        "container_notes.anchoring_urls.umd_ext",
        "harvest_ready_anchoring_urls.umd_ext",
        "storage.anchoring_urls.umd_ext",
        "tips_by_stage.establishment[0].anchoring_urls.umd_ext",
        "tips_by_stage.harvest[0].anchoring_urls.umd_ext"
      ],
      "source_context": "As an alternative, grow mint in a bottomless container (remove the base of a utilitarian plastic pot) and plant the container into the ground, leaving about an inch of the container above the soil line. This forces the runners up and over the container lip where they can be seen and trimmed off before they root into soil on the other side.",
      "source_context_available": true,
      "tool_first_6gram": "the container above the soil line"
    },
    {
      "crop_slug": "mint",
      "field_path": "fertilizer.notes_seasoned",
      "our_prose": "Mint is a light feeder and is easily overfed. Utah State suggests about one teaspoon of a balanced 16-16-16 fertilizer per plant in early spring as growth resumes; a compost-rich bed often needs no more than a light topdressing. Go easy, because rich soil and heavy nitrogen push soft, floppy stems and thin the essential oils that carry mint's flavor and aroma, a point Wisconsin Horticulture makes for mints grown in lean ground. In a container, where frequent watering leaches nutrients, a dilute liquid feed every few weeks through the growing season keeps the leaves green.",
      "run_normalized": "16 16 16 fertilizer per plant",
